For a boost along the $1$-axis, the coordinates transform as $\begin{aligned}
  x'^1&=\gamma(x^1-vx^0), &
  x'^0&=\gamma(x^0-vx^1), \\
  x'^2&=x^2, & x'^3&=x^3.\end{aligned}$ Apply the same transformation separately to both tensor indices. For the component parallel to the boost, the board begins with a product: $\begin{aligned}
  (A'^0B'^1)
  =\gamma^2\bigl(
     A^0B^1-vA^0B^0-vA^1B^1+v^2A^1B^0
   \bigr).\end{aligned}$ Replacing each product by the corresponding tensor component and then using antisymmetry, $F^{00}=F^{11}=0$ and $F^{10}=-F^{01}$, gives $F'^{01}
  =\gamma^2(1-v^2)F^{01}
  =F^{01}.$ Thus the electric field component parallel to the motion is unchanged. The parallel magnetic component behaves similarly.

